s='<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Final frozen NL RH portfolio · independent mathematical review</title><style>'+CSS+'</style><main><h1>Final NL RH portfolio: independent mathematical review</h1><p class="muted">Final frozen source · 21 November 2026 UTC · historical 900-second review retained separately</p>'
s+='''<div class="intro"><div class="tiles"><div class="tile"><b>Mathematical verdict</b><br>The final four-family portfolio survives written review, with one explicit weighted-tail proof repair.</div><div class="tile"><b>Substantive change</b><br>B₅ beats every admissible variable-height member of the complete published CH Corollary 1.2 family.</div><div class="tile"><b>Actual new replays</b><br>Quadrature, all 28 analytic cells, local crossing, small weighted audit and the entire C++ finite interval reproduced.</div></div><p>This review covers the final PNT B₃/B₄/B₅ bounds and their transports; both logarithmic interval tests including the expanded finite band and count; weighted signed and coupled consequences; and the RH/finite-height Ramanujan chain. It does not infer final validity from the historical checkpoint. The final files were copied and hashed before review; all originals remain unchanged.</p><p><b>Proof boundary.</b> These are supported written analytic derivations with separate native numerical evidence. The supplied local Lean crossing theorem and rational cell checker do not constitute a full Lean proof of the zeta/BT/analytic propagation. No new source-first C/C++/CUDA program verification is claimed.</p><p>All RH implications use the pinned Mathlib RiemannHypothesis. The finite-height Ramanujan route uses the published verification through 3·10¹² and does not assume full RH. There is no GRH, pair correlation or zero-simplicity assumption. No discovery worker was contacted and no other arm output was opened.</p></div>'''
s+='<article class="valid"><span class="badge">PNT · WRITTEN B₄ AND B₅ VALID</span><h2>The final parabolic refinement is materially stronger than B₃</h2><p><b>Project statement.</b> '+draft('PNT.md','116–225')+' supplies no new Blueprint label or Lean theorem for these analytic bounds. With L=log x≥35 and v=L/2−log L:</p><div class="eq">B₄(x)=√x[(v²+1.846v+1.28+L)/(2π)+0.14],<br>B₅(x)=√x[(v²+1.662v+1.148+L)/(2π)+0.14],<br>|ψ(x)−x|≤B₅(x)&lt;B₄(x)&lt;B₃(x).</div><p><b>Primary analytic inputs.</b> '+src('LN',3,'Lee–Nosal (8), p. 3')+' and the original '+src('BPT',4,'Brent–Platt–Trudgian Corollary 1, p. 4')+' give</p><div class="eq">N(T)=T/(2π)log(T/(2π))−T/(2π)+⅞+R(T),<br>|R(T)|≤0.28log T &nbsp; (T≥2π).</div><p>The integrated explicit formula is in '+src('LN',4,'Lee–Nosal (13), p. 4')+' and '+src('DGM',2,'Dudek–Grenié–Molteni, p. 2')+'. The exact B₄/B₅ formulas are new project deductions, rather than statements imported from those sources.</p><p><b>Review.</b> The signed and regularized Mellin calculations check. The B₄ coefficients follow from J₀=3/2−γ<sub>E</sub>&lt;0.923 and J₁=½(3/2−γ<sub>E</sub>)²+5/8−π²/24&lt;0.64. For B₅, the correct absolute parabolic moments are below 0.831 and 0.574. The imaginary displacement of the Fourier argument is explicitly controlled; it is not legitimate to substitute the real-axis profile without that correction, and the final draft includes the correction.</p><details><summary>Transform and correction details</summary><div class="eq">φ(y)=3(sin y−y cos y)/y³, &nbsp; φ(0)=1,<br>∫₀∞φ(y)y<sup>s−1</sup>dy=3Γ(s)cos(πs/2)/[(1−s)(3−s)] &nbsp; (0&lt;s&lt;1).</div><p>Subtract 1/s and expand at zero. The signed regularized moments are 4/3−γ<sub>E</sub> and 13/9−4γ<sub>E</sub>/3+γ<sub>E</sub>²/2−π²/24. Adding twice the negative-part moments gives the absolute moments actually required for the zero sum. The damping/limit argument is valid because |u|⁻ˢ is integrable against the compact parabolic density for 0&lt;s&lt;1.</p><p>The two shifted averages bracket right-continuous ψ, including its prime-power jumps. Their main-term loss is at most xδe²ᵟ, δ=L/(2π√x). Under RH the multiplier is e<sup>±ρδ</sup>φ(δ(γ−i/2)). Along the vertical shift, |φ′(y+it)|≤3eᵟᐟ²min(1,4/y²), so its normalized contribution is below 0.000271 uniformly. The other exponentiation/main-shift corrections remain below the stated combined allowance. N(12)=0 follows from the integer-valued zero-count bound, and the density-discrepancy constant is below 0.130. Thus 0.14 has adequate slack on the entire unbounded domain.</p><p>The signed θ and π transports retain their exact domains, ordinary endpoints and Li₂ normalization. The elementary power-count bounds Q between 0.99√x/L and 1.1√x/L at L≥35 and the signed π consequences check. B₅ changes lower-order terms; its leading ψ coefficient remains 1/(8π).</p></details></article>'
s+='<article class="valid"><span class="badge">STRONG SAME-RH COMPARISON · COMPLETE FUNCTION FAMILY</span><h2>B₅ improves the entire published CH Corollary 1.2 family</h2><p><b>Project correspondence.</b> The actual candidate is '+draft('PNT.md','165–225')+'. The following comparison is reviewer work after the final lock; it is not discovery-arm credit and is not a global optimality claim.</p><p><b>Exact primary statement.</b> '+src('CH',3,'Chirre–Helfgott, arXiv:2512.15709v1, Corollary 1.2, p. 3')+' assumes RH through T≥10⁷ and x&gt;max(T,10⁹), and gives the complete bound</p><div class="eq">|ψ(x)−x(π/T)coth(π/T)|≤πx/(T−1)<br>+√x[(log(T/(2π)))²/(2π)−log(T/(2π))/(6π)].</div><p>For a bound around x, add the exact centering transfer [ (π/T)coth(π/T)−1 ]x. No csch term appears in this corollary; no remainder from a general-kernel theorem has been omitted.</p><p><b>Comparison with every height, rather than one convenient height.</b> Put r=log(T/(2π)). The complete normalized source envelope is</p><div class="eq">C<sub>CH</sub>(L,T)=πeᴸᐟ²/(T−1)+(r²−r/3)/(2π)<br>+[(π/T)coth(π/T)−1]eᴸᐟ²<br>≥F<sub>L</sub>(r)=½e<sup>L/2−r</sup>+(r²−r/3)/(2π).</div><p>The inequality only drops positive terms, so it supplies a lower bound even after optimizing T. F is strictly convex. Its unique unconstrained minimizer lies between r₀=L/2−log L+log π and L/2. Throughout that interval F″≥μ=½+1/π. Hence</p><div class="eq">min<sub>r∈ℝ</sub>F<sub>L</sub>(r)≥F<sub>L</sub>(r₀)<br>−[log L−log π+1/6]²/[π(π+2)].</div><p>Subtract B₅/√x. With a=2logπ−1/3−1.662 and b=(logπ)²−logπ/3−1.148, the resulting lower difference is</p><div class="eq">g(L)=[a(L/2−log L)+b]/(2π)−0.14<br>−[log L−logπ+1/6]²/[π(π+2)].</div><p>Exact rational enclosures give g(35)&gt;0.0666 and g′(35)&gt;0.012. The positive first part of g′ increases, and its subtracted logarithm-over-L part decreases for L≥35. Therefore g increases, proving</p><div class="eq">C<sub>CH</sub>(L,T)−B₅(x)/√x &gt;0.0666<br>for every L≥35 and every admissible T.</div><p><b>Impact.</b> The historical B₃ frontier objection does not apply to final B₅. B₄ alone is slightly weaker than optimized CH near L=35, while B₅ is stronger there and everywhere in its stated domain. This compares the explicit published Corollary 1.2 family; it does not prove that no other use of CH’s general theorem or another source is stronger.</p></article>'
s+='<article class="valid"><span class="badge">RIGOROUS QUADRATURE · PARAMETERS AND REMAINDERS CHECKED</span><h2>The parabolic integral certificates reproduce with their continuous error bounds</h2><p><b>Project declaration.</b> '+draft('parabola.py','7–74')+', '+draft('Intervals.md','182–211')+' and '+draft('PNT.md','181–211')+' use h=0.002, endpoint A=100, 45 alternating Taylor terms for each small rotation, and outward rounding to a 2⁻²⁵⁶ dyadic grid. There is no full Lean proof of these trigonometric/variation certificates.</p><p><b>Mathematical basis.</b> The profile is the Fourier transform of 1−u² on [−1,1]. The transform identities are derived in the draft; no primary source is claimed for these exact numerical constants. Their proof reduces to the explicitly displayed derivative bounds and positive analytic tails, not to unverified numerical zero data.</p><table><tr><th>Integral</th><th>Replayed rigorous upper ceiling</th><th>Allowance used</th></tr><tr><td>π⁻¹∫|f(y)|dy</td><td>1.238656</td><td>1.24</td></tr><tr><td>π⁻¹∫|f(y)|log y dy</td><td>0.491739</td><td>0.50</td></tr><tr><td>Regularized absolute moment of φ/y</td><td>0.829290</td><td>0.831</td></tr><tr><td>Regularized absolute log moment of φ/y</td><td>0.570302</td><td>0.574</td></tr></table><p><b>Review.</b> The Taylor next-term bounds, exact interval rotation, tangent upper bounds for log, all grid boundaries, and positive tail remainders check. The first two rectangles have continuous variation allowances (h/2)·4.54 and (h/2)·7.7. The negative [0,1] log contribution is bounded above by −4/3+2/135, with the inequality direction correctly reversed by log y&lt;0. The signed negative-part moments are zero below 2 and receive their separate variation and tail allowances. Thus the computation encloses continuous integrals.</p><details><summary>Explicit variation reductions</summary><div class="eq">|f′(y)|≤4y/15 &nbsp;(0≤y≤2),<br>|f′(y)|≤4/y²+12/y³+12/y⁴ &nbsp;(y≥2),<br>∫|f′|&lt;4.54, &nbsp; ∫|f′|log⁺y&lt;6.1,<br>∫₁∞|f|/y&lt;1.6.</div><p>The weighted log variation is therefore below 7.7. For the negative φ/y moments on [2,∞), the corresponding derivative integrals are below 1.7 and 3. The tails after 100 use |φ|≤3/y²+3/y³. Each interval upper at a midpoint dominates the actual integrand there; its possible dependency overestimation only enlarges the result.</p><p>The supplied Python programs use <code>assert</code> for acceptance, so optimized Python removes their gates. Their supplied normal executions were freshly reproduced. Reviewer checks and receipts use explicit failure checks and pass under default, −O and −OO. This robustness limitation is separate from the validity of the fixed-parameter integral bounds.</p></details></article>'
s+='<article class="valid"><span class="badge">INTERVALS · FULL PARAMETER DOMAIN VALID</span><h2>The final open-interval band reaches exp(199), with a count coefficient of 2.43</h2><p><b>Project statement.</b> '+draft('Intervals.md','156–230')+' has no new Blueprint or Lean theorem. For L=log x≥24, put D₁=0.465L−0.93log L−0.25. For every D₁≤D≤2L, δ=D/√x, a=xe⁻ᵟ and b=xeᵟ, it asserts</p><div class="eq">#{p prime:a&lt;p&lt;b}&gt;[(4/3)(D−D₁)+0.04]√a/log a.</div><p><b>Primary comparators.</b> '+src('DGM',1,'DGM Theorem 1.1, p. 1')+' gives symmetric radii (½+2/log x)√x log x for one prime and adds √x log x to each radius for √x primes. '+src('CMS',5,'CMS Theorem 5, p. 5')+' gives a prime in [u,u+(22/25)√u log u] for every u≥4. Their '+src('CMS',5,'Theorem 3, p. 5')+' gives an asymptotic count coefficient bounded by 3/C⁺(B)&lt;63/25 when α=1.</p><p><b>Review.</b> The main weighted integral is at least (4/3)D. The absolute zero-density mass and log moment from the preceding card give 1.24log(1/(2πδ))+0.50. The R discrepancy includes its zero endpoint and costs &lt;0.011, prime powers cost &lt;0.0155, and the trivial term costs &lt;0.001. The conservative remaining surplus exceeds 0.04 throughout D₁≤D≤2L. The test vanishes at both endpoints, including prime powers.</p><p>For D=D₁, the exact conversion ratio is</p><div class="eq">(b−a)/(√a log a)<br>=(2D₁/L)eᵟᐟ²(sinh δ/δ)/(1−δ/L)&lt;0.88<br>(24≤L≤200).</div><p>The map L↦L−δ increases and its image contains [24,199]. Thus every exp(24)≤u≤exp(199) has an open prime-containing subinterval of (u,u+0.88√u log u), with a positive quantitative count. Setting D=D₁+3L/4 gives more than √x primes; the finite endpoint factors retain positive slack.</p><details><summary>Exact scope of the asymptotic count comparison</summary><p>The full width for that count choice has leading coefficient 2(0.465+0.75)=2.43. For any fixed ε&gt;0, increasing D by εL gives a limiting normalized lower count greater than 1 and width coefficient 2.43+2ε. Hence the infimum of coefficients that yield liminf count/√u&gt;1 is at most 2.43.</p><p>This improves both the displayed CMS numerical bound 63/25=2.52 and the ordinary-prime refinement 3·0.8358=2.5074 in '+src('CPL',2,'Chirre–Pereira Júnior–de Laat §1.3, p. 2')+'. It does not establish improvement over the unknown exact value 3/C⁺(B), nor does it prove a global priority claim. For prime gaps without a count target, its asymptotic coefficient is 0.93, which is worse than the published &lt;0.8358 gap coefficient. The finite 0.88 comparison remains meaningful beyond the much smaller verified gap range.</p><p>The older triangle, wide-interval signed transform and finite inverse example also retain valid endpoint/domain handling. The inverse example imports Büthe only within 10¹⁹ and is a deduction, not a priority claim.</p></details></article>'
s+='<article class="minor"><span class="badge">WEIGHTED · LOCAL PROOF REPAIR REQUIRED, DISPLAYED CONSTANTS SURVIVE</span><h2>Restore the full-prime normalization tail before forming coupled errors</h2><p><b>Actual project fragment.</b> '+draft('Weighted.md','83–100, 177–224, 275–285')+' defines T<sub>A</sub> using p≤x, but uses it as the entire correction after replacing E<sub>A</sub> by E. The omission enters the new coupled identity and cross-family identity as well as the earlier single weighted formula. No corresponding full analytic Lean theorem is supplied.</p><p><b>Primary formulation.</b> '+src('BFK',2,'BFKNgWilk (6), p. 2')+' defines the first Mertens constant using all primes, and '+src('BFK',31,'Proposition 29, (154), p. 31')+' gives a C<sub>A</sub> inequality. '+src('BFK',12,'Proposition 13, (55), p. 12')+' treats the other integrated ψ tail. The exact normalization therefore requires</p><div class="eq">S<sub>A</sub>=Σ<sub>p prime,k≥2,pᵏ&gt;x</sub>(log p)/pᵏ,<br>A−log x−E=D/x−F(x)+S<sub>A</sub>,<br>T<sub>W</sub>=Σ<sub>p≤x,k≥2,pᵏ&gt;x</sub>1/(kpᵏ),<br>B<sub>out</sub>=Σ<sub>p&gt;x,k≥2</sub>1/(kpᵏ).</div><p><b>Concrete repair and impact.</b> Let C<sub>all</sub>=Σ<sub>p,k≥2,pᵏ&gt;x</sub>[(log p)/pᵏ](1−L/log(pᵏ)). After the common ψ endpoint cancels, the prime-power contributions are exactly C<sub>all</sub> for D<sub>A</sub>−LD<sub>B</sub>, and C<sub>all</sub>+LB<sub>out</sub> for D<sub>A</sub>−LD<sub>W</sub>. The draft’s all-prime integral estimate bounds C<sub>all</sub>; W needs the extra LB<sub>out</sub>≤1.045/x.</p><p>After scaling by L√x, the repaired W upper allowance is</p><div class="eq">2.09+7.875e⁻ᴸᐟ⁶+0.047(3+4/L)<br>+q e⁻ᴸᐟ²+1.045Le⁻ᴸᐟ² &lt;2.39 &nbsp;(L≥24).</div><p>Every error term decreases, and the exact endpoint check passes. The lower bound remains valid. Thus both strict coupled conclusions survive:</p><div class="eq">0.13/(L√x)&lt;D<sub>A</sub>−LD<sub>s</sub>&lt;2.39/(L√x),<br>D<sub>s</sub>∈{D<sub>B</sub>,D<sub>W</sub>}, &nbsp;L≥24.</div><p>For the cross-family statement, use the full S<sub>A</sub> in the exact identity; the original a₋ and a₊ already bound that full tail. Its strict shift between 1.89 and 2.22 in units √x/L therefore survives too. This is a proof-bookkeeping error, not a counterexample to a displayed bound.</p></article>'
s+='<article class="valid"><span class="badge">WEIGHTED · SIGNED AND EARLY-ONSET CONSEQUENCES CHECK</span><h2>The cancellation is valid; the old 1.20 benchmark is not a frontier by itself</h2><p><b>Project statement.</b> '+draft('Weighted.md','13–39, 135–175, 226–260')+' gives signed tails, symmetric 1.20 envelopes, an early onset exp(5.7) on the Büthe window, and a separate exact small audit.</p><p><b>Exact recent primary baseline.</b> '+src('BFK',31,'BFKNgWilk Proposition 29(ii), (154), p. 31')+' and '+src('BFK',13,'Proposition 13(ii), (57), p. 13')+' state, for x≥2,</p><div class="eq">|C<sub>A</sub>−L+γ<sub>E</sub>|≤|D|/x+J₁+log(2π)/x,<br>|∫ₓ∞[(1+log t)/(t²log²t)](ψ(t)−t)dt|<br>≤[(L+1)/L²]J₁+[(L+4)/L³]J₂+2.15/(xL),<br>J<sub>m</sub>=Σ<sub>ρ</sub>x<sup>Reρ−1</sup>/|Imρ|<sup>m+1</sup>.</div><p>Under RH, N(12)=0 and Z&lt;0.047 imply J₁≤0.047(1+1/576)/√x and J₂≤J₁/12. Combined with the same elementary power-tail estimate, these source inequalities already recover the 1.20 envelopes, with slightly smaller paddings. The two product orientations follow by exponentiation; '+src('BFK',33,'BFKNgWilk Theorem 31, (168), pp. 33–34')+' and '+src('BFK',34,'Theorem 32, (172), p. 34')+' give the published product/log correspondence.</p><p><b>Review.</b> The final factor &lt;⅓ comparison with all four Lee–Nosal Corollary 1.3 bounds is correct when retaining eᵛ−1≤v/(1−v). The early-onset bounds use Büthe’s 0.94 only through 10¹⁹; the lower θ bounds used inside improper tails are separately extended globally under RH, so the proof does not silently truncate those tails. The all-real low audit reproduces and is unconditional on its stated small intervals. Its worst upper ratios on [50,300) are 0.803413, 0.659945, 0.657787 and 0.635648, all below 0.82.</p><p>The new coupled and cross-family statements preserve a common oscillatory boundary before taking absolute values. They are useful valid deductions after the preceding repair. Integrated zero tails and positive square bias already have substantial published precedent; no universal novelty or best-constant claim follows.</p></article>'
s+='<article class="valid"><span class="badge">RAMANUJAN · WRITTEN FINITE-HEIGHT CHAIN VALID</span><h2>The centered Logan and BT argument supports the finite window through exp(665)</h2><p><b>Project statement.</b> '+draft('Ramanujan.md','8–30, 98–215')+' claims G(x)=exπ(x/e)/log x−π(x)²&gt;0 on (κ,exp(665)] without full RH, plus a full-RH continuation. '+draft('EnvelopeData.lean','20–56')+' checks rational cell data; '+draft('RationalEnvelope.lean','44–95')+' has the polynomial consumer. These do not prove the analytic error inputs.</p><p><b>Primary assumptions and formula.</b> '+src('PTZERO',1,'Platt–Trudgian, arXiv:2004.09765v1, abstract and Theorem 1')+' verifies all zeros 0&lt;γ≤3·10¹² on the line. '+src('JOHN',3,'Johnston §2, logarithmic/Logan smoothing')+' supplies the source context. The explicit Brun–Titchmarsh input was checked in its original '+src('MV',3,'Montgomery–Vaughan Theorem 2, printed p. 121, (1.12)')+':</p><div class="eq">π(M+N)−π(M)&lt;2N/log N &nbsp;(M&gt;0,N&gt;1).</div><p><b>Review.</b> The normalized nonnegative Logan density, its entire transform, the complex high-zero estimates without RH above H, the half-first moment I₁(36)/(2sinh36)&lt;0.066, and both smoothing transfers check. The centered BT argument pays a half-moment rather than the entire support; its tiny t₀ enlargement preserves the interval and ensures log h≥L−35. It gives</p><div class="eq">|θ(x)−x|≤118√x+d(L)x,<br>d(L)=1.59·10⁻¹²L/(L−35)+2.2·10⁻¹⁴ &nbsp;(L≥100).</div><p>The Abel transfer keeps a source-valid shifted bound in the integral from the fixed exp(12) anchor. Its normalized π allowance is</p><div class="eq">ε<sub>center</sub>(u)=118(1+3/u)e⁻ᵘᐟ²+d(u)<br>+1.2(1.24·10⁻¹¹)/u+82ue⁻ᵘ &nbsp;(u≥100).</div><p>The increasing algebraic part of u⁴ε and decreasing exponential part justify every closed cell, including the use of L−1 at the lower count argument. All 28 rational cell calculations reproduce. The final centered cell [650,665] has RG margin at least 0.009051. The li factorial barriers, exact cancellation and RH terminal cell also check, yielding the claimed full-RH onset L≥38.95 and RG≥1+14/L+142/L² for L≥60.</p><details><summary>Coverage and native finite evidence</summary><p>The independent C++ replay covers every real x in [38,358,837,683,1,630,650,286,564], with 388,741,288 coarse passes, 1,743 refined cells, 291,842 fine points and directed integer G lower bound 10,786,299. It completed in 143.810 seconds, with peak resident memory 3,130,728 KiB. The interval logic checks initial points and all prime jumps; between them the positive term is nondecreasing. The fixed-point log/e directions and 128-bit bounds check.</p><p>The independent quotient-count replay gives π(38,358,837,682)=1,644,673,232 and π(14,111,427,770)=632,207,037, and the root enclosure</p><div class="eq">38,358,837,682.21792636514465 &lt;κ<br>&lt;38,358,837,682.21792636514466.</div><p>The branch is strictly increasing and π(x/e) is constant throughout [k,k+1]. The local right endpoint is handled separately; k+1 is composite. The native interval overlaps the Büthe cells starting at exp(28.12), these overlap shifted-height cells beginning at exp(42), and shifted-height cells overlap centered cells beginning at exp(101). No interval gap remains. Full RH continues the result beyond the finite window. G(κ)=0 makes κ itself a counterexample to the strict inequality, as claimed.</p><p>'+draft('CertifiedCrossing.lean','52–92')+' proves the local crossing conditional on two actual Lean sieve output equalities. '+draft('NativeCrossing.lean','8–15')+' supplies them by native_decide. This mathematical review inspected those boundaries but did not independently recompile the Lean modules. It did rerun the Python/C++ numerical programs. Compiler/runtime and the full C++ semantics remain outside source-first verification.</p></details></article>'
s+='<article class="context"><span class="badge">RAMANUJAN FRONTIER · STRONGER SOURCE ADAPTATION</span><h2>The exp(665) extension is valid relative to Johnston; CH already supplies stronger inputs</h2><p><b>Project claim.</b> '+draft('Ramanujan.md','276–304')+' explicitly compares its finite endpoint with '+src('JOHN',10,'Johnston Theorem 5.1, pp. 10–11')+', which states Ramanujan positivity on [38,358,837,683,exp(103)]. That named-source extension is correct; it is not automatically a best-known-method claim.</p><p><b>Exact stronger primary statement.</b> '+src('CH',3,'Chirre–Helfgott Corollary 1.3, p. 3')+' gives unconditionally, for every x≥1,</p><div class="eq">|ψ(x)−x|≤[π/(3·10¹²)]x+113.67√x.</div><p><b>Separate source-correct transfer.</b> Put α=π/(3·10¹²). '+src('PTTHETA',2,'Platt–Trudgian Corollary 2, p. 2')+' supplies ψ−θ≤1.00000075√x+3x¹ᐟ³. Hence, for log x≥12:</p><div class="eq">|θ(x)−x|≤αx+[113.67+1.00000075+3e⁻²]√x<br>&lt;αx+116√x.</div><p>With the same exp(12) anchor and direct integral inequalities, one obtains</p><div class="eq">ε<sub>CH</sub>(u)=116(1+3/u)e⁻ᵘᐟ²<br>+α(1+1.2/u)+82ue⁻ᵘ &nbsp;(u≥12).</div><p>This is pointwise smaller than the project’s shifted allowance, and for u≥100 it is also smaller than the centered allowance. The exact source constant α&lt;1.05·10⁻¹² is below the centered floor. Feeding a conservative version with coefficient 118 and α rounded upward to 1.05·10⁻¹² into the same polynomial consumer gives a fresh last-cell margin 0.403269 on [650,665], versus 0.009051 for the project’s own centered route.</p><p><b>Impact.</b> The centered argument is a valid independent derivation and a real extension of Johnston’s displayed finite theorem. A claim that the exp(665) range defeats the strongest credible same-premise source adaptation is not supported. This adaptation and comparison are reviewer work after the lock, not credit to either discovery arm. No new farther endpoint is claimed here. The gap to '+src('AX',4,'Axler Theorem 1.1, printed p. 1150')+' at exp(3158.442) remains.</p></article>'
s+='<article class="context"><span class="badge">POST-LOCK ROOT COMPARISON · SEPARATE ATTRIBUTION</span><h2>The supplied other-arm A function is uniformly above B₅</h2><p>The root supplied only the following locked comparison function after the final discovery lock; no other arm output was read in this review:</p><div class="eq">A(L)=0.158L+[(v+k)²−(v+k)/3]/(2π),<br>k=log(10/π), &nbsp;v=L/2−log L, &nbsp;L≥34.</div><p>On L≥35 the exact full-function difference is</p><div class="eq">A−B₅/√x=(0.158−1/(2π))L<br>+[(2k−1/3−1.662)v+k²−k/3−1.148]/(2π)−0.14.</div><p>Using 1.15&lt;k&lt;1.16 and log L≤L/8, its lower bound is</p><div class="eq">[0.158−0.88575/(2π)]L<br>+[(1.15)²−1.15/3−1.148]/(2π)−0.14 &gt;0.4.</div><p>The slope is positive and the endpoint L=35 checks by exact rational enclosures. Adding the supplied nonnegative 0.332 or 0.821 allowances only increases A. This is a function comparison, not an independent verification of the other arm’s claims, code or proof consumption. The root separately reported that a later source-first coefficient component was not consumed by its completed 640 kernel proof; no such component is credited here.</p></article>'
s+='''<article class="context"><span class="badge">FINAL GATE AND EVIDENCE</span><h2>Precise completion boundary</h2><table><tr><th>Requested family</th><th>Written mathematics</th><th>Strong-source conclusion</th></tr><tr><td>PNT B₄/B₅ and signed transports</td><td>Supported throughout stated domains</td><td>B₅ improves all variable-height CH Corollary 1.2 envelopes; no global optimality claim</td></tr><tr><td>Parabolic intervals/counts</td><td>Supported for full D range; finite exp(199) conversion checks</td><td>Improves stated numerical count coefficients, not unknown C⁺ optimum or asymptotic gap record</td></tr><tr><td>Weighted/coupled consequences</td><td>Supported after full-prime tail proof repair</td><td>Prior integrated-tail formulas recover 1.20; no global priority proof for coupled packaging</td></tr><tr><td>Ramanujan finite/full-RH chain</td><td>Supported with explicit native/imported-source boundary</td><td>Extends Johnston’s stated exp(103) range; stronger published CH inputs recover at least the same window</td></tr></table><p>The specified final mathematical population has passed the written-review gate, subject to the one local proof repair. No material false-bound counterexample was found. This is not an exhaustive strict issue census for the entire IEANTN network. One minor proof-bookkeeping finding is recorded separately from positive validity findings and frontier comparisons.</p><p>Full analytic Lean formalization and new source-first verification are not established by the artifacts. Native computations, kernel rational checking, actual Lean function semantics, imported source computations, and mathematical source claims remain separate. The source programs’ optimized-Python acceptance limitation remains a concrete reproducibility repair, not evidence of false integral values.</p><p>Evidence: <a href="DRAFT_PIN.json">frozen file hashes</a> · <a href="SOURCE_PIN.json">primary source pins</a> · <a href="REVIEW_MATH_CHECKS.json">21 reviewer endpoint checks and adapted cells</a> · <a href="REVIEW_MATH_CHECKS_O1.json">−O receipt</a> · <a href="REVIEW_MATH_CHECKS_O2.json">−OO receipt</a> · <a href="review_math.py">reviewer reproduction</a> · <a href="parabola-replay.out">quadrature replay</a> · <a href="bounds-replay.out">28-cell replay</a> · <a href="finite-ram-replay.out">full finite replay</a> · <a href="finite-ram-resources.out">resource receipt</a> · <a href="last-real-replay.out">crossing replay</a> · <a href="weighted-small-replay.out">small weighted replay</a>.</p></article></main></html>'''
